  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A Nor'easter will begin weaken tonight as it departs to the\nnortheast Monday.\n\nThe Nor'easter will start weakening tonight as it pivots near \nthe Delaware Bay. This will lead to decreasing rain chances \ntonight, although some patches of light rain or drizzle may \npersist. Lows will be in the 50s to near 60.\n\nAs the blocking ridge departs, the low will be able to start\nprogressing northeastward Monday while continuing to weaken.\nWinds gusts will be much lower than the weekend, and most\nlocations should remain dry. However, cloud cover will likely \nremain stubbornly in place in a broad moisture shield left\nbehind by the upper low. The current forecast calls for highs in\nthe upper 60s and lower 70s, but would not be surprised if they\nare a bit lower if clouds are thick. As winds shift more \nnorthwesterly, some upslope showers or drizzle will be possible \nin the Alleghenies.\n \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Warm, tranquil weather through midweek before \nchances for showers return Friday and into next weekend.\n\nAs the Nor'easter and accompanying longwave trough continue to pull \naway from the Atlantic coast Tuesday and Wednesday, mid/upper \nheights begin to build in earnest. These above average 500-mb \nheights coincide with a transient upper ridge swinging eastward \ntoward the Atlantic Seaboard. Subsidence accompanying this regime \nfavors dry and warming conditions. However, the moisture left behind \nby the low may become trapped beneath the subsidence inversion, so \nTuesday will likely feature more clouds than sun. Likely more \nsunshine on Wednesday as surface winds turn southerly. Some fair \nweather cumulus likely develop Wednesday and Thursday as instability \nincreases a bit through the day with dry conditions through this \nperiod. More sunshine brings warming daytime temperatures through \nthe work week. Global ensembles show fairly high agreement on high \ntemperatures rising into the 70s Wednesday and perhaps the mid 80s \non Thursday. \n\nHeading into the weekend, temperatures and the potential for rain \nremains somewhat uncertain, mainly revolving around how far south \nand how quickly a cold front can push into the Mid-Atlantic. \nCurrently, global ensemble members favor a later arrival with rain \nchances and daytime temperatures rising Friday and through the \nbeginning of the weekend. However, temperatures are generally \ndependent on shower and cloud coverage associated with this front. \nOverall, the farther and sooner this front pushes southward into our \nregion, the quicker we could see temperatures returning to \nseasonable next weekend.",
